.TH FILECRYPT 1 "November 2026" "Custom Shell" "Custom Shell Commands"
.SH NAME
filecrypt \- encrypt or decrypt a file with a key
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B filecrypt
.RB ( \-e | \-d )
.RB [ \-k
.IR key ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B filecrypt
encrypts or decrypts
.I file
using a key chosen by the user.
The key is hashed into a seed for a pseudo random keystream, and every byte
of the file is XORed with the next keystream byte.
Running the same key over the encrypted file restores the original.
.PP
Both files are accessed with
.BR mmap (2).
The key is kept in memory locked with
.BR mlock (2)
so it is never written to swap, and it is wiped with
.BR explicit_bzero (3)
before the command exits.
When the key is typed at the prompt, terminal echo is turned off.
.PP
Encrypted files start with a 12 byte header: the text
.B FCR1
followed by an 8 byte check value derived from the key.
This lets
.B filecrypt
reject a wrong key or a file it did not create before writing any output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-e
Encrypt
.IR file .
.TP
.B \-d
Decrypt
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-k " key"
Use
.I key
as the key (up to 255 characters).
If left out, the key is read from standard input, with echo turned off when
standard input is a terminal.
Keys given with
.B \-k
can be seen by other users in the process list, so the prompt is safer.
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Write the result to
.IR output .
By default, encrypting adds
.B .enc
to the file name, and decrypting removes it (or adds
.B .dec
if the name does not end in
.BR .enc ).
The output file is created with mode 0600.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose. Show input and output sizes, time taken, speed, and whether the key
memory was locked.
.TP
.B \-h
Show a short help message.
.SH SIGNALS
Pressing Ctrl+C (SIGINT) stops the command, deletes the partial output file,
wipes the key, and exits with status 130.
The shell's original SIGINT handler is restored afterward.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Bad options, a file error, a wrong key, or an input that is not a filecrypt
file.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted by Ctrl+C.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Encrypt a file, typing the key at the prompt:
.B filecrypt \-e notes.txt
.TP
Decrypt it back to notes.txt:
.B filecrypt \-d notes.txt.enc
.TP
Encrypt with a key on the command line and show timing:
.B filecrypt \-e \-v \-k secret \-o backup.enc photo.jpg
.SH NOTES
This is a teaching cipher for a course project.
It is not a replacement for real encryption such as AES.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mmap (2),
.BR mlock (2),
.BR ftruncate (2),
.BR msync (2),
.BR termios (3),
.BR sigaction (2)
